@@ -0,0 +1,212 @@
+# Terminal Pong specification
+
+The implementation targets Python 3.11 or newer and uses only the Python
+standard library. Coordinates are measured in terminal character cells. A
+positive `x` direction is right and a positive `y` direction is down.
+
+## Requirements
+
+1. **Package, command, and dependencies.** The repository shall contain the
+   importable package files `pong/__init__.py`, `pong/core.py`,
+   `pong/terminal.py`, and `pong/__main__.py`. Running `python3 -m pong` shall
+   start the game and exit with the status returned by
+   `pong.__main__.main()`. Production code shall import no third-party
+   packages. The game is a local two-player game: the left player uses W/S and
+   the right player uses O/L. Source and on-screen text shall not require
+   Unicode support.
+
+2. **Public constants and units.** `pong` shall export these constants with
+   exactly these values:
+
+   | Name | Value | Meaning |
+   | --- | ---: | --- |
+   | `SCREEN_WIDTH` | `80` | rendered columns |
+   | `SCREEN_HEIGHT` | `24` | rendered rows |
+   | `COURT_WIDTH` | `80` | court columns, numbered 0 through 79 |
+   | `COURT_HEIGHT` | `22` | court rows, numbered 0 through 21 |
+   | `LEFT_PADDLE_X` | `2` | left paddle column |
+   | `RIGHT_PADDLE_X` | `77` | right paddle column |
+   | `PADDLE_HEIGHT` | `5` | paddle cells |
+   | `PADDLE_SPEED` | `1` | cells per simulation tick |
+   | `BALL_HORIZONTAL_SPEED` | `0.5` | cells per simulation tick |
+   | `BALL_INITIAL_VERTICAL_SPEED` | `0.3` | cells per simulation tick |
+   | `BALL_DEFLECT_SPEED` | `0.5` | cells per simulation tick |
+   | `TICK_RATE` | `30` | simulation ticks per second |
+   | `TICK_SECONDS` | `1 / 30` | seconds per simulation tick |
+   | `SERVE_TICKS` | `30` | active ticks before a serve |
+   | `WINNING_SCORE` | `5` | points needed to win |
+   | `MAX_CATCH_UP_TICKS` | `5` | maximum updates in one terminal-loop pass |
+
+   The playable vertical ball range is the inclusive interval 1 through 20.
+   A paddle top is clamped to the inclusive interval 1 through 16. The paddle
+   collision planes are `x = 3` (left) and `x = 76` (right).
+
+3. **Public model and real starting state.** `pong` shall export string enums
+   `Side` (`LEFT = "left"`, `RIGHT = "right"`) and `Phase`
+   (`SERVE = "serve"`, `PLAYING = "playing"`, `GAME_OVER = "game_over"`).
+   It shall export these frozen dataclasses, with the fields in the shown
+   order:
+
+   ```python
+   @dataclass(frozen=True)
+   class Controls:
+       left_move: int = 0
+       right_move: int = 0
+       toggle_pause: bool = False
+       restart: bool = False
+       quit: bool = False
+
+   @dataclass(frozen=True)
+   class GameState:
+       left_paddle_y: int
+       right_paddle_y: int
+       ball_x: float
+       ball_y: float
+       ball_vx: float
+       ball_vy: float
+       left_score: int
+       right_score: int
+       phase: Phase
+       serve_ticks_remaining: int
+       serve_direction: Side
+       paused: bool
+       winner: Side | None
+       tick: int
+       points_played: int
+   ```
+
+   `Controls` shall raise `ValueError` unless each move is `-1`, `0`, or `1`.
+   `initial_state() -> GameState` shall return a new value equal to:
+   `(8, 8, 39.5, 10.5, 0.0, 0.0, 0, 0, Phase.SERVE, 30, Side.RIGHT,
+   False, None, 0, 0)` in field order. Public state transitions return new
+   values and never mutate an existing state.
+
+4. **Key parsing.** `parse_keys(keys: str) -> Controls` shall be pure and
+   case-insensitive. W/S request left movement -1/+1 and O/L request right
+   movement -1/+1. If both directions for one paddle occur in the same
+   string, that paddle's movement is 0, regardless of order or repetition.
+   An odd number of spaces sets `toggle_pause`; an even number does not.
+   R sets `restart`; Q or control-C (`"\x03"`) sets `quit`. Unknown characters,
+   including terminal escape sequences, are ignored. Parsing does not perform
+   I/O.
+
+5. **Core transition, paddles, pause, and restart.**
+   `advance(state: GameState, controls: Controls = Controls()) -> GameState`
+   shall be pure. Restart has highest priority: if `controls.restart` is true,
+   return `initial_state()` irrespective of every other field. In `SERVE` or
+   `PLAYING`, a pause toggle has next priority and only flips `paused`; that
+   call does not move anything or increment `tick`. While paused, all controls
+   other than restart or a pause toggle are ignored and the state is
+   unchanged. `controls.quit` has no core effect. On every other call in
+   `SERVE` or `PLAYING`, first move each paddle by its requested move times
+   `PADDLE_SPEED`, clamp its top to 1..16, then update the serve or ball, and
+   increment `tick` exactly once. In `GAME_OVER`, every call without restart
+   returns the unchanged state, including a pause toggle.
+
+6. **Serve timeline.** During `SERVE`, the ball remains at `(39.5, 10.5)` with
+   zero velocity while `serve_ticks_remaining` is decremented. The call that
+   changes it from 1 to 0 changes the phase to `PLAYING` and sets velocity but
+   does not yet move the ball. Horizontal velocity is `+0.5` when
+   `serve_direction` is `RIGHT`, otherwise `-0.5`. Vertical velocity is
+   `+0.3` when `points_played` is even and `-0.3` when it is odd. Thus the
+   first 29 calls leave one serve tick, the 30th launches at the center, and
+   the 31st moves the ball.
+
+7. **Ball movement and walls.** A `PLAYING` update computes candidate
+   `x = ball_x + ball_vx` and `y = ball_y + ball_vy`. Before paddle and goal
+   handling, a candidate above 1 is mirror-reflected to `2 - y` and vertical
+   velocity becomes positive; a candidate below 20 is mirror-reflected to
+   `40 - y` and vertical velocity becomes negative. Equality with 1 or 20
+   does not reflect. Generated game states must keep the ball within the
+   vertical playable range.
+
+8. **Paddle collisions.** After wall handling, a left collision occurs only
+   when the ball is moving left, its old `x` is greater than 3, its candidate
+   `x` is at or left of 3, and candidate `y` is in the half-open interval
+   `[left_paddle_y, left_paddle_y + 5)`. A right collision is the symmetric
+   test for a right-moving ball crossing 76 from an old `x` below 76. Mirror
+   the candidate horizontal coordinate around the plane (`x = 6 - x` on the
+   left; `x = 152 - x` on the right), and set horizontal velocity away from
+   the paddle with magnitude 0.5. Let `relative_y = candidate_y - paddle_y`.
+   Set vertical velocity to `-0.5` if `relative_y < 5/3`, to `0.0` if
+   `5/3 <= relative_y < 10/3`, and to `+0.5` otherwise. A ball outside the
+   paddle interval continues toward the goal. These tests and intervals are
+   symmetric for both paddles.
+
+9. **Scoring, subsequent serves, and winning.** After wall and paddle
+   handling, a candidate `x < 0` awards one point to the right player; a
+   candidate `x > 79` awards one point to the left player. Equality with 0 or
+   79 is not a score. Increment `points_played`, reset the ball to the center
+   with zero velocity, preserve both paddle positions, and set
+   `serve_direction` toward the player who conceded the point. If the new
+   score is below 5, enter `SERVE` with 30 remaining ticks and no winner. If
+   it is 5, enter `GAME_OVER`, use zero remaining ticks, and set `winner` to
+   the scoring side. The first player to 5 wins; there is no win-by-two rule.
+
+10. **Determinism over time.** The core shall use no clock, randomness, global
+    mutable state, terminal, or network. Repeated default transitions from
+    the real starting state have these checkpoints: after 30 calls the ball
+    is launched from center at `(0.5, 0.3)` velocity; after 50 calls its
+    position is `(49.5, 16.5)`; after 110 calls the left score is 1 and the
+    next 30-tick serve is waiting at center; after 550 calls the state is
+    `GAME_OVER` with score 5-0, `Side.LEFT` as winner, `points_played == 5`,
+    and `tick == 550`.
+
+11. **Pure renderer.** `render(state: GameState) -> str` shall perform no I/O
+    and return exactly 24 lines of exactly 80 ASCII characters, joined with
+    `"\n"` and with no trailing newline. Line 1 is a centered status; lines
+    2..23 are the court; line 24 is centered controls text. Centering uses
+    `str.center(80)`. The court uses `+` corners, `-` top/bottom edges, `|`
+    side edges and paddle cells, spaces inside, and `o` for the ball. Paddles
+    occupy their configured column and five rows beginning at their top.
+    Render the ball at `(floor(ball_x + 0.5), floor(ball_y + 0.5))`, with the
+    ball taking precedence over every other court glyph, including a paddle or
+    border. The controls text is exactly
+    `"W/S: left  O/L: right  SPACE: pause  R: restart  Q: quit"`. The status
+    is `f"Left {left_score}  Right {right_score}  {phase_text}".center(80)`,
+    where `phase_text` is `PAUSED` when paused, `SERVE {ceil(remaining / 30)}s`
+    in `SERVE`, `PLAY` in `PLAYING`, or
+    `"{winner.name} WINS - R TO RESTART"` in `GAME_OVER`. Enum `name` is used,
+    so winner names are uppercase. Rendering the same state repeatedly is
+    byte-for-byte stable.
+
+12. **Terminal boundary and lifecycle.** `pong.terminal` shall publicly
+    define a `typing.Protocol` named `Terminal` with methods
+    `get_size() -> tuple[int, int]`, `enter() -> None`, `restore() -> None`,
+    `read_keys() -> str`, `write(text: str) -> None`,
+    `monotonic() -> float`, and `sleep(seconds: float) -> None`. It shall
+    provide a standard-library `SystemTerminal` implementing non-blocking key
+    reads, flushed writes, and reversible raw/cbreak terminal setup, and
+    `run(terminal: Terminal | None = None) -> int`, defaulting to
+    `SystemTerminal`. Before entering terminal mode, `run` checks for at least
+    80 columns and 24 rows. If too small, it writes exactly
+    `"Pong requires a terminal of at least 80 columns by 24 rows.\n"`, does
+    not call `enter` or `restore`, and returns 1.
+
+    With sufficient size, `run` calls `enter`, hides the cursor and clears the
+    screen with `"\x1b[2J\x1b[?25l"`, immediately writes the initial render
+    at home (`"\x1b[H"`), and then runs fixed 1/30-second updates. Keys read
+    since the previous update are parsed together. Quit exits without another
+    update. The loop uses the injected monotonic clock and sleep, performs at
+    most `MAX_CATCH_UP_TICKS` overdue updates per pass, applies a key batch
+    only to the first such update, drops older excess lag, and renders after
+    updates. `SystemTerminal.read_keys` signals input EOF by raising
+    `EOFError`. In a `finally` block `run` writes `"\x1b[?25h\n"` and calls
+    `restore`, including after control-C, EOF, or any exception; unexpected
+    exceptions are re-raised after cleanup. Normal Q, `KeyboardInterrupt`,
+    or `EOFError` exit returns 0. Key presses are discrete one-cell requests
+    (normal terminal key repeat supplies continued movement), not persistent
+    held-key state.
+
+13. **Module entry point.** `pong.__main__` shall expose
+    `main(terminal: Terminal | None = None) -> int`, which delegates to
+    `pong.terminal.run(terminal)`. Its module-execution guard shall raise
+    `SystemExit(main())`. Consequently tests and embedding code can exercise
+    exactly the same path as `python3 -m pong` using a fake terminal, without
+    accessing a real terminal or clock.
+
+## Public imports
+
+For convenience, `pong.__init__` shall re-export all constants in requirement
+2 and `Side`, `Phase`, `Controls`, `GameState`, `initial_state`, `parse_keys`,
+`advance`, and `render`. Terminal-specific names remain in `pong.terminal`.
